# What never travels to the Docker daemon.
#
# Docker matches a pattern without a slash against the top level only -- the opposite of
# `.gitignore`, where a bare name matches at every depth. That difference is why this file was
# quietly doing almost nothing: `node_modules` excluded one directory out of the twenty-eight in
# this workspace, and `.next` excluded a directory that does not exist while leaving the 2.4 GB
# one under `apps/web` in place. Everything meant to match at any depth needs `**/`.
#
# The build only ever copies `apps/`, `packages/` and five named root files, so what is here is
# not the last line of defence -- `deploy/Dockerfile` is. This file decides what crosses to the
# daemon at all, which is a question of how long every build takes and of what a build stage can
# read even when no stage copies it.

# Version control, agent workspaces and editor state. `.claude/` and `.worktrees/` each hold
# whole checkouts of this repository with their own installed dependencies.
.git
.github
.claude
.codex
.worktrees

# Anything a previous build wrote. All of it is regenerated inside the image, and a tree
# installed on Windows carries binaries an Alpine container cannot execute.
#
# `.next*` rather than `.next` because the verification stack writes to `.next-verify`, and on
# this machine that directory alone was a gigabyte of turbopack cache -- the single largest
# thing left in the context after everything above was excluded. It was found by measuring the
# context, not by reading the tree: `.gitignore` already knew about it and this file did not.
**/node_modules
**/.next*
**/.turbo
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Test and report output.
**/coverage
**/playwright-report
**/test-results
.e2e

# Secrets, at every depth, which is the part that was not merely slow. The old top-level `.env*`
# left `apps/web/.env.local` in the context, and `next build` reads that file: a `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
# value in it is inlined into the bundle that ships.
#
# The one exception is named at the top level only, and deliberately so. Written as
# `!**/.env.example` it also matched the copies inside `.claude/worktrees/`, and a re-included
# file drags its parent directories back in: the whole 1.1 GB tree reappeared in the context
# because of one example file. Verified, not reasoned about -- a probe stage that copies the
# context and lists what arrived.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example

# Design material that no stage builds from.
stitch_avant_business_ecosystem
